      "routeSummary": "Waterfalls, Root Bridges & Safari",
      "overviewText": "This [Meghalaya Trip](https://indiatour.live/meghalaya) is one's for people who don't want to choose between hills and wildlife.\n\nMost Meghalaya trips end at [Dawki](https://indiatour.live/attractions/dawki-river) or Mawlynnong. We stretch this one two days further, out of the hills entirely, and into [Kaziranga](https://indiatour.live/attractions/kaziranga-national-park) for a jeep safari. Seven days. One small group. Two very different landscapes, back to back.\n\nThe first half runs the classic [Meghalaya route](https://indiatour.live/tours/meghalaya-group-trip). Shillong to start: pine forests, hill markets, a slow warm-up into the trip. [Laitlum Canyon](https://indiatour.live/attractions/laitlum-canyon) on day two, where the valley just falls away below you and the clouds move through it like they're in a hurry. Then Cherrapunji, wet enough to feel it in the air the moment you arrive.\n\nDay three is the trek, down to the [Double Decker Living Root Bridge](https://indiatour.live/attractions/double-decker-living-root-bridge), then out to Seven Sisters Falls. By day four and five, the pace drops. Dawki's river, still enough to look painted on. Mawlynnong's bamboo walkways. A last waterfall stop at [Krang Suri](https://indiatour.live/attractions/krang-suri-falls) before the trip turns.\n\nThat's where day six comes in. We drive you straight out of the hills and into Kaziranga's grassland, a different world entirely. Day seven, you're up before sunrise for a jeep safari, tracking rhinos through the morning mist, before we get you to Guwahati airport.\n\nNobody leaves this one saying they wish they'd added something.\n\n\n### 📍 Where This Meghalaya Trip Takes You\n\n1. **[Shillong](https://indiatour.live/attractions/shillong):** Your first two nights. Pine-covered hills, a capital that doesn't feel like one, markets and cafes open well into the evening. Easiest place on the route to settle into the trip's pace.\n2. **Laitlum Canyon:** \"End of the hills\" in Khasi, and the view backs that up. The land drops off sharply into a valley that stays half-covered in cloud most of the day. We time this one for golden hour.\n3. **[Cherrapunji \\(Sohra\\)](https://indiatour.live/attractions/cherrapunji):** One of the wettest spots on the planet, and it shows in how fast the weather turns here. Waterfalls, limestone caves, and root bridge country all sit within a short drive of each other.\n4. **[Double Decker Living Root Bridge](https://indiatour.live/attractions/double-decker-living-root-bridge):** Two tiers, grown from rubber tree roots over generations, still used daily by locals. Getting there means a proper trek — steps, streams, dense forest the whole way down.\n5. **[Seven Sisters Falls](https://indiatour.live/attractions/seven-sisters-falls):** Seven streams dropping together off the same cliff face. Heaviest right after monsoon. Viewed from across the valley, so no trek required for this one.\n6. **[Mawsmai Cave](https://indiatour.live/attractions/mawsmai-caves):** A short limestone cave walk, 20–30 minutes, no gear needed. A breather between the bigger days.\n7. **[Mawlynnong](https://indiatour.live/attractions/mawlynnong-village):** Asia's cleanest village — bamboo-paved paths, a living root bridge on the village edge, and a bamboo skywalk with a view that stretches into Bangladesh on a clear day.\n8. **Dawki:** The Umngot river's home turf. The water's clear enough that boats look like they're hovering in photos. This is your base for the water activities.\n9. **Krang Suri / Phe Phe Falls:** A turquoise pool, off the usual tourist track, calm enough to swim in. The last stop before the trip turns toward Kaziranga.\n10. **[Kaziranga National Park](https://indiatour.live/attractions/kaziranga-national-park):** A UNESCO-listed grassland and the world's biggest population of one-horned rhinos. We get you in on a jeep at first light, when the animals are most active and the mist hasn't lifted yet.\n\n### ✅ Things To Do In Meghalaya On This Trip\n\n✓ Trekking down to the Double Decker Living Root Bridge through Cherrapunji's forest\n✓ Cliff jumping into the [Umngot river](https://indiatour.live/attractions/umngot-river) at Dawki\n✓ Snorkelling in water clear enough to see straight to the riverbed\n✓ Boating across Dawki's glass-still river\n✓ Walking through Mawsmai Cave's limestone passages\n✓ A bonfire by the river at Dawki, weather permitting\n✓ Catching Laitlum Canyon at sunrise or sunset\n✓ A dawn jeep safari through Kaziranga, tracking rhinos, elephants, and swamp deer\n\n### Why This Trip Over the Standard Meghalaya Route\n\n- **It doesn't stop at the hills:** You get the full Meghalaya circuit and a Kaziranga safari without booking two separate trips.\n- **Nothing's left out:** Root bridges, waterfalls, caves, the Umngot river, a safari — one itinerary covers all of it.\n- **You're not doing this alone:** Small group format, built-in company, shared costs, none of the usual solo-trip logistics.\n- **We run the whole thing:** Stays, transport, permits, safari bookings, a trip captain on call throughout. You just need to show up.\n- **The pace is built to not burn you out:** Trekking days are followed by slower ones, and Kaziranga's flat grassland at the end is a genuine change of scenery, not more of the same.\n\n**Start planning your [Meghalaya Trip](https://indiatour.live/meghalaya/enquiry) NOW!**",
